Round 602 — KnowledgeOS Architecture Definition + C4 + DDD Implementation Blueprint

We have reached the point where continuing to add isolated concepts would be counterproductive. The next step is to turn the accumulated theory into a real software architecture that can be implemented, tested, audited, and evolved.

The central architectural conclusion from Rounds 597–601 is:

$$ \boxed{ KnowledgeOS = \text{typed} + \text{contract-governed} + \text{provenance-preserving} + \text{regime-aware} + \text{assessment-oriented} + \text{assurance-controlled} } $$

The most important decision I recommend now is:

KnowledgeOS should initially be implemented as a modular monolith with a strong internal domain architecture, not as microservices.

The theory is not mature enough to justify distributed runtime boundaries. We should discover those boundaries empirically through the reference implementation.

1. First: what exactly are we building?

KnowledgeOS is not:

a database;
an AI system;
a knowledge graph;
a probability engine;
a truth engine;
a workflow engine;
an ontology engine;
a decision engine.

It is better described as:

An epistemic infrastructure platform that preserves authoritative information, semantic context, provenance, temporal history and formal contracts, and derives regime-relative assessments, determinations and assurance artifacts from them.

Formally:

$$ \boxed{ K_{t+1}=Update(K_t,e_t,C_t) } $$

while:

$$ \boxed{ A_t=Assess(K_t,Q_t,C_t,\Gamma_t) } $$

and:

$$ \boxed{ Cert_t=Assure(A_t,\Gamma_t,C_t) } $$

So:

