                     ▼
               Conformance

The Python implementation becomes the executable specification.

The Java system must pass conformance tests against it.

35. This is a very important concept: executable specification

An Executable Specification is a small implementation whose primary purpose is to define expected behavior unambiguously for a bounded formal model.

It is not necessarily production software.

For KnowledgeOS:

$$ \boxed{ ReferenceCalculus = ExecutableSpecification } $$

Then:

$$ Conforms(S,R,D) \iff \forall x\in D: S(x)\equiv R(x) $$

where:

\(S\) = production implementation;
\(R\) = reference implementation;
\(D\) = test domain.

This is one of the strongest ways to prevent theoretical drift.

36. Testing architecture

I recommend six test levels.

Level 1 — Unit

Pure domain rules.

Level 2 — Property-based

Generate many valid/invalid states.

Level 3 — Metamorphic

Verify transformation invariants.

Level 4 — Reference conformance

Java vs Python reference.

Level 5 — Adversarial

Try to break invariants deliberately.

Level 6 — ML assurance

IID, OOD, calibration, drift, adversarial.

37. Property-based example

For projection:

$$ \pi(\pi(K))=\pi(K) $$

if projection is defined as idempotent.

Generate thousands of finite states.

Check:

project(project(K)) == project(K)

This is far more powerful than one hand-written example.

38. TPP property test

For every generated state pair:

If:

$$ \pi(K_1)=\pi(K_2) $$

then require:

$$ Z(K_1)=Z(K_2) $$

for a claimed TPP.

If a counterexample occurs:

TPP = FAIL
Counterexample = (K1,K2)

This makes TPP executable.

39. Mutation testing

We should deliberately break the implementation.

Examples:

remove provenance
change regime
drop temporal field
invert Unknown/False
ignore dependency
skip assumption
mutate projection

The invariant suite should detect the corruption.

This is particularly important for KnowledgeOS because correctness depends heavily on negative distinctions.

40. Security and epistemic integrity

A KnowledgeOS implementation has a special security property beyond conventional application security:

Epistemic integrity

The system must prevent unauthorized mutation of:

evidence;
provenance;
historical assessment;
contract versions;
certificates;
regime definitions.

Therefore:

Write Evidence
    ↓
Immutable event
    ↓
Assessment

rather than:

UPDATE evidence SET valid = false

without historical record.

